te **only one entry** to describe the collection process."},"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditAnswerEvent","followUpUuids":{"changed":false},"label":{"changed":false},"metricMeasures":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-04-09T13:00:18Z","entityUuid":"3f78cf3e-9016-4303-9439-297c69168a93","parentUuid":"0c88f0a5-92cf-4e82-b528-6bdcf3a0bc8c","uuid":"1a1445d4-edcf-49ec-a13a-761e837415fc"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"itemTemplateQuestionUuids":{"changed":false},"questionType":"ListQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":false},"text":{"changed":true,"value":"A distribution is a specific representation or version of a dataset that is made available for access or download. A single dataset may have multiple distributions that vary in format, structure, language, media type, level of detail, or other characteristics. This concept follows the widely used [W3C DCAT metadata standard](https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/#Class:Distribution), which distinguishes between the abstract idea of a dataset and its concrete forms of availability. Distributions enable the attachment of specific metadata—such as file size or license—to each version. Importantly, a distribution may be defined even if it is not ultimately published.\n\nCreate a separate entry for each distribution and clearly specify which dataset it is associated with."},"title":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-04-09T13:00:19Z","entityUuid":"1f45710f-6481-4aa9-9c75-bd59ff70691b","parentUuid":"7fccc342-deaa-4105-bfdf-43b872291c86","uuid":"1dac1dbc-cba5-480a-9742-69e6518fcb21"},{"content":{"eventType":"MoveQuestionEvent","targetUuid":"d6a2ece7-c068-48b4-bfaa-b032f432afd8"},"createdAt":"2025-04-09T13:00:20Z","entityUuid":"7f5a30bf-c6d5-49a6-9dc2-fba2094eb541","parentUuid":"30fda3ae-3b4d-486c-b626-fb585fa92571","uuid":"62898e49-2d1e-4096-841d-097b6366b0d4"},{"con