:"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"integrationUuid":{"changed":false},"questionType":"IntegrationQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":false},"text":{"changed":true,"value":"Provide the used PID system. You will be presented with suggestions from [FAIRsharing](https://fairsharing.org/), but you also have the option to manually enter the PID system that is not listed there."},"title":{"changed":false},"variables":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:34Z","entityUuid":"3ab3a652-09cd-4c20-abff-48bbe5fe844c","parentUuid":"6856ec6e-9cb3-4310-bb03-c24b9ed8901a","uuid":"5813c801-c1cb-459d-9bdb-538c3eab1854"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"questionType":"ValueQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":true,"value":[]},"text":{"changed":false},"title":{"changed":false},"validations":{"changed":false},"valueType":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:35Z","entityUuid":"e2dc07f9-0cea-456a-b628-79e6b1a076bb","parentUuid":"11ee0c25-3017-4bd2-949d-c97439dffcb2","uuid":"67981743-10f0-4f4f-811e-bc844ba0fcc4"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":true,"value":[{"key":"json.key","value":"isCompletePublished"}]},"answerUuids":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"questionType":"OptionsQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":false},"text":{"changed":false},"title":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:36Z","entityUuid":"626a7119-b9a7-40df-ae71-6825cb970c68","parentUuid":"b91d4cfb-5240-4ae8-8e08-fcfc47e66cc3","uuid":"66013292-5d8d-4fe0-b949-f26fd383664f"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":true,"value":[{"key":"json.key","value":"partialPublishedDetails"}]},"eventType":"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"questionType":"ValueQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":false},"text":{"changed":false},"title":{"changed":false},"validations":{"changed":false},"valueType":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:37Z","entityUuid":"d268a1a8-0831-4d03-995d-c385436e9ba9","parentUuid":"5e2639c4-69c0-4fe7-8684-1ca65b73ffdc","uuid":"13f18f7b-1029-4367-9830-0f2e00969d80"},{"content":{"allowCustomReply":{"changed":false},"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditIntegrationEvent","integrationType":"ApiIntegration","name":{"changed":false},"requestAllowEmptySearch":{"changed":false},"requestBody":{"changed":false},"requestHeaders":{"changed":false},"requestMethod":{"changed":false},"requestUrl":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplate":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplateForSelection":{"changed":false},"responseListField":{"changed":false},"testQ":{"changed":true,"value":"isbn"},"testResponse":{"changed":true,"value":{"request":{"body":null,"headers":[{"key":"Accept","value":"application/json"},{"key":"Authorization","value":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiKey]"}],"method":"GET","url":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiUrl]/v2/search?registry=standard&record_type=identifier_schema&q=isbn&status=ready"},"response":{"body":"[{\"id\":140,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry Specification Format\",\"homepage\":\"http://opensmiles.org/opensmiles.html\",\"abbreviation\":\"SMILES\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.qv4b3c\",\"description\":\"This format is an open specification version of the SMILES language, a typographical line notation for specifying chemical structure. It is hosted under the banner of the Blue Obelisk project, with the intent to solicit contributions and comments from the entire computational chemistry community.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":1360,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Transcription Factor Class Schema\",\"homepage\":\"http://www.edgar-wingender.de/TFClass_schema.html\",\"abbreviation\":\"TFClass Schema\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.yIYItV\",\"description\":\"The TFClass Schema is an identifier schema for classifying transcription factors according to a six-level schema, four of which are abstractions according to different criteria, while the fifth level represents TF genes and the sixth individual gene products. The first four levels of the TFClass schema are Superclass, Class, Family and Subfamily, with Subfamily an optional category. Two lower levels represent physical entities (i.e. genes and gene products) and have been termed Genera and Species (or \\\"molecular species\\\", to avoid confusion with biological species).\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":3904,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Research Activity Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://raid.org/\",\"abbreviation\":\"RAiD\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.dc702a\",\"description\":\"Research Activity Identifier (RAiD) is an identifier mechanism designed for research projects and activities. It is persistent and connects existing PIDs for researchers, institutions, outputs and tools together to create a timeline of research activity which makes connecting digital infrastructure, reporting on impact and establishing data provenance clear and easy. RAiD is one of the Identifier Services owned and offered by the Australia Research Data Commons (ARDC), and can be accessed for machine-to-machine service by API integration, using a RAiD service point designated platform, or by manually minting via the RAiD user Dashboard. \",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":715,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Open Researcher and Contributor iD Identifier Schema\",\"homepage\":\"https://support.orcid.org/hc/en-us/articles/360006897674-Structure-of-the-ORCID-Identifier\",\"abbreviation\":\"ORCID iD Identifier Schema\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.OrNi1L\",\"description\":\"The ORCID Identifier Schema is an https URI with a 16-digit number that is compatible with the ISO Standard (ISO 27729), also known as the International Standard Name Identifier (ISNI), e.g. https://orcid.org/0000-0001-2345-6789 Initially ORCID iDs will be randomly assigned by the ORCID Registry from a block of numbers that will not conflict with ISNI-formatted numbers assigned in other ways. ORCID iDs always require all 16 digits of the identifier; they can not be shortened to remove leading zeros if they exist. No information about a person is encoded in the ORCID iD. The identifiers were designed to be usable in situations where personally-identifiable information should/can not be shared. Also, since the ORCID iD is designed to be a career-long identifier, no information that can change over a person's career is embedded in the iD, e.g., country, institution, field of study.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":317,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Archival Resource Key Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://arks.org/\",\"abbreviation\":\"ARK Identifier\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.f928f1\",\"description\":\"Archival Resource Keys (ARKs) serve as persistent identifiers, or stable, trusted references for information objects. Among other things, they aim to be web addresses (URLs) that don’t return 404 Page Not Found errors. The ARK Alliance is an open global community supporting the ARK infrastructure on behalf of research and scholarship. End users, especially researchers, rely on ARKs for long term access to the global scientific and cultural record. Since 2001 some 12.3 billion ARKs have been created by over 1700 organizations — libraries, data centers, archives, museums, publishers, government agencies, and vendors. They identify anything digital, physical, or abstract. ARKs are open, mainstream, non-paywalled, decentralized persistent identifiers that can be created by an organization as soon as it is registered with a NAAN (Name Assigning Authority Number). Once registered, an ARK organization can create unlimited numbers of ARKs and publicize them via the n2t.net global resolver or via their own local resolver.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":1319,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Persistent Uniform Resource Locator\",\"homepage\":\"https://purl.archive.org/help\",\"abbreviation\":\"PURL\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.3e603c\",\"description\":\"PURLs are Web addresses or Uniform Resource Locators (URLs) that act as permanent identifiers in the face of a dynamic and changing Web infrastructure. Instead of resolving directly to Web resources (documents, data, services, people, etc.). PURLs allow third party control over both URL resolution and resource metadata provision. A URL is simply an address of a resource on the World Wide Web. A Persistent URL is an address on the World Wide Web that causes a redirection to another Web resource. If a Web resource changes location (and hence URL), a PURL pointing to it can be updated. A user of a PURL always uses the same Web address, even though the resource in question may have moved. PURLs may be used by publishers to manage their own information space or by Web users to manage theirs; a PURL service is independent of the publisher of information. When a user retrieves a PURL they will be redirected to the current location of the resource. When an author needs to move a page they can update the PURL to point to the new location. PURLs with a common prefix are grouped together into domains. Each domain has a single maintainer who can add new PURLs to the domain and make changes to existing PURLs within the domain. Each PURL has a target and status code or type. The target specifies where the PURL redirects to. The type is a status code from HTTP specification. The default PURL type is \\\"302 Found\\\", meaning that the object of the request was found elsewhere. This is the status code that should be used if there is not a reason to use a different one.\\n\\nA partial PURL is a special type which will match the beginning of a URL. The PURL resolver will match as much of a PURL as it can and append the remainder to the end of the resolved URL. This reduces the need to create multiple PURLs to handle all of the resources that share a common location. For more on partials, see the section below the following table.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"}]","contentType":"application/json","responseType":"SuccessTypeHintResponse","status":200}}},"testVariables":{"changed":true,"value":{"record_type":"identifier_schema","registry":"standard","status":"ready"}},"variables":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:38Z","entityUuid":"90958723-2ffa-4b75-80a5-354f433da8ee","parentUuid":"2563c25b-4c5c-4840-a3f4-09e5de5ded4f","uuid":"61eb1535-7b16-4840-b096-e3993525e9a7"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":false},"answerUuids":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditQuestionEvent","expertUuids":{"changed":false},"questionType":"OptionsQuestion","referenceUuids":{"changed":false},"requiredPhaseUuid":{"changed":false},"tagUuids":{"changed":false},"text":{"changed":true,"value":null},"title":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:39Z","entityUuid":"5876f65b-be89-4200-9584-ed01e9b2aadc","parentUuid":"7a12e6c6-09f8-4ee2-a6fc-99c4946c3de9","uuid":"44e55703-7f86-479b-9077-8e8a5ce992be"},{"content":{"annotations":[],"eventType":"AddReferenceEvent","label":"","referenceType":"URLReference","url":""},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:40Z","entityUuid":"0a89f8a7-facb-4bc7-854c-734e5b110c0c","parentUuid":"5876f65b-be89-4200-9584-ed01e9b2aadc","uuid":"7cc3ae05-d959-473a-a295-582cbea2b921"},{"content":{"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditReferenceEvent","label":{"changed":true,"value":"CC0 License"},"referenceType":"URLReference","url":{"changed":true,"value":"https://creativecommons.org/public-domain/cc0/"}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:41Z","entityUuid":"0a89f8a7-facb-4bc7-854c-734e5b110c0c","parentUuid":"5876f65b-be89-4200-9584-ed01e9b2aadc","uuid":"4eefafca-66b3-4efb-b215-048ca682e900"},{"content":{"allowCustomReply":{"changed":false},"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditIntegrationEvent","integrationType":"ApiIntegration","name":{"changed":false},"requestAllowEmptySearch":{"changed":false},"requestBody":{"changed":false},"requestHeaders":{"changed":false},"requestMethod":{"changed":false},"requestUrl":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplate":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplateForSelection":{"changed":false},"responseListField":{"changed":false},"testQ":{"changed":true,"value":"doi"},"testResponse":{"changed":true,"value":{"request":{"body":null,"headers":[{"key":"Accept","value":"application/json"},{"key":"Authorization","value":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiKey]"}],"method":"GET","url":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiUrl]/v2/search?registry=standard&record_type=identifier_schema&q=doi&status=ready"},"response":{"body":"[{\"id\":1332,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Digital Object Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.doi.org/the-identifier/resources/handbook/\",\"abbreviation\":\"DOI (ISO 26324:2025)\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.hFLKCn\",\"description\":\"The digital object identifier (DOI) system originated in a joint initiative of three trade associations in the publishing industry (International Publishers Association; International Association of Scientific, Technical and Medical Publishers; Association of American Publishers). The system was announced at the Frankfurt Book Fair 1997. The International DOI® Foundation (IDF) was created to develop and manage the DOI system, also in 1997. The DOI system was adopted as International Standard ISO 26324 in 2012 and updated in 2025. The DOI system implements the Handle System and adds a number of new features. The DOI system provides an infrastructure for persistent unique identification of objects of any type. The DOI system is designed to work over the Internet. A DOI name is permanently assigned to an object to provide a resolvable persistent network link to current information about that object, including where the object, or information about it, can be found on the Internet. While information about an object can change over time, its DOI name will not change. A DOI name can be resolved within the DOI system to values of one or more types of data relating to the object identified by that DOI name, such as a URL, an e-mail address, other identifiers and descriptive metadata. The DOI system enables the construction of automated services and transactions. Applications of the DOI system include but are not limited to managing information and documentation location and access; managing metadata; facilitating electronic transactions; persistent unique identification of any form of any data; and commercial and non-commercial transactions. The content of an object associated with a DOI name is described unambiguously by DOI metadata, based on a structured extensible data model that enables the object to be associated with metadata of any desired degree of precision and granularity to support description and services. The data model supports interoperability between DOI applications. The scope of the DOI system is not defined by reference to the type of content (format, etc.) of the referent, but by reference to the functionalities it provides and the context of use. The DOI system provides, within networks of DOI applications, for unique identification, persistence, resolution, metadata and semantic interoperability.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":4495,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"DDI Agency Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://registry.ddialliance.org/\",\"abbreviation\":null,\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.cfe09b\",\"description\":\"The DDI Agency Identifier is part of a free globally-unique identifier system for metadata-producing organizations. In concert with the DDI Agency Registry, the DDI Agency Identifier acts as both a namespace in a globally-unique id and a pointer for distributed service resolution.\\nBased on ISO/IEC 11179 Part 6 International Registration Data Identifiers (11179 IRDIs), DDI agency identifiers are used as the Registration Authority (11179 RA) in a IRDI and in DDI URNs.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":3904,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Research Activity Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://raid.org/\",\"abbreviation\":\"RAiD\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.dc702a\",\"description\":\"Research Activity Identifier (RAiD) is an identifier mechanism designed for research projects and activities. It is persistent and connects existing PIDs for researchers, institutions, outputs and tools together to create a timeline of research activity which makes connecting digital infrastructure, reporting on impact and establishing data provenance clear and easy. RAiD is one of the Identifier Services owned and offered by the Australia Research Data Commons (ARDC), and can be accessed for machine-to-machine service by API integration, using a RAiD service point designated platform, or by manually minting via the RAiD user Dashboard. \",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"},{\"id\":4538,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"International Generic Sample Number\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.igsn.org\",\"abbreviation\":\"IGSN (Generic)\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.c7f365\",\"description\":\"An IGSN ID is a globally unique and persistent identifier for physical samples in all scientific fields. An IGSN ID can be applied to an individual sample, an aggregation of samples, or to a feature-of-interest (the real-world feature that the sample is taken from). From 2021, IGSN IDs are functionally DOIs and are registered with metadata encoded in the DataCite Metadata Schema. The IGSN ID, previously known as an \\\"International GeoSample Number\\\", naming schema has changed, to reflects the expanding scope and utilization as a partnership agreement between DataCite and the IGSN e.V.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\"}]","contentType":"application/json","responseType":"SuccessTypeHintResponse","status":200}}},"testVariables":{"changed":false},"variables":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:42Z","entityUuid":"90958723-2ffa-4b75-80a5-354f433da8ee","parentUuid":"2563c25b-4c5c-4840-a3f4-09e5de5ded4f","uuid":"21fb2157-1df7-43cc-bbba-37358388456d"},{"content":{"allowCustomReply":{"changed":false},"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditIntegrationEvent","integrationType":"ApiIntegration","name":{"changed":false},"requestAllowEmptySearch":{"changed":false},"requestBody":{"changed":false},"requestHeaders":{"changed":false},"requestMethod":{"changed":false},"requestUrl":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplate":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplateForSelection":{"changed":false},"responseListField":{"changed":false},"testQ":{"changed":true,"value":"handle"},"testResponse":{"changed":true,"value":{"request":{"body":null,"headers":[{"key":"Accept","value":"application/json"},{"key":"Authorization","value":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiKey]"}],"method":"GET","url":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiUrl]/v2/search?registry=standard&record_type=identifier_schema&q=handle&status=ready"},"response":{"body":"[{\"id\":1538,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Handle\",\"homepage\":\"http://handle.net\",\"abbreviation\":\"hdl\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.0b7e54\",\"description\":\"The Handle System is the Corporation for National Research Initiatives's proprietary registry assigning persistent identifiers, or handles, to information resources, and for resolving \\\"those handles into the information necessary to locate, access, and otherwise make use of the resources\\\". As with handles used elsewhere in computing, Handle System handles are opaque, and encode no information about the underlying resource, being bound only to metadata regarding the resource. Consequently, the handles are not rendered invalid by changes to the metadata.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":\"https://hdl.handle.net/{$id}\"},{\"id\":4142,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Persistent Identifier Consortium for eResearch\",\"homepage\":\"https://doc.pidconsortium.net/docs/generate\",\"abbreviation\":\"ePIC Identifier\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.83ded0\",\"description\":\"ePIC is to set up and maintain a reliable joint service for registering, storing and resolving persistent identifiers based on handles for the research community. ePIC was founded in 2009 by a consortium of European partners in order to provide PID services for the European Research Community, based on the handle system, for the allocation and resolution of persistent identifiers. The consortium signed a Memorandum of Understanding aiming to provide long term reliability for the PID services. Meanwhile ePIC is also an international consortium and open to partners from the research community worldwide.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":null},{\"id\":1319,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Persistent Uniform Resource Locator\",\"homepage\":\"https://purl.archive.org/help\",\"abbreviation\":\"PURL\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.3e603c\",\"description\":\"PURLs are Web addresses or Uniform Resource Locators (URLs) that act as permanent identifiers in the face of a dynamic and changing Web infrastructure. Instead of resolving directly to Web resources (documents, data, services, people, etc.). PURLs allow third party control over both URL resolution and resource metadata provision. A URL is simply an address of a resource on the World Wide Web. A Persistent URL is an address on the World Wide Web that causes a redirection to another Web resource. If a Web resource changes location (and hence URL), a PURL pointing to it can be updated. A user of a PURL always uses the same Web address, even though the resource in question may have moved. PURLs may be used by publishers to manage their own information space or by Web users to manage theirs; a PURL service is independent of the publisher of information. When a user retrieves a PURL they will be redirected to the current location of the resource. When an author needs to move a page they can update the PURL to point to the new location. PURLs with a common prefix are grouped together into domains. Each domain has a single maintainer who can add new PURLs to the domain and make changes to existing PURLs within the domain. Each PURL has a target and status code or type. The target specifies where the PURL redirects to. The type is a status code from HTTP specification. The default PURL type is \\\"302 Found\\\", meaning that the object of the request was found elsewhere. This is the status code that should be used if there is not a reason to use a different one.\\n\\nA partial PURL is a special type which will match the beginning of a URL. The PURL resolver will match as much of a PURL as it can and append the remainder to the end of the resolved URL. This reduces the need to create multiple PURLs to handle all of the resources that share a common location. For more on partials, see the section below the following table.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":null},{\"id\":1332,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Digital Object Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.doi.org/the-identifier/resources/handbook/\",\"abbreviation\":\"DOI (ISO 26324:2025)\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.hFLKCn\",\"description\":\"The digital object identifier (DOI) system originated in a joint initiative of three trade associations in the publishing industry (International Publishers Association; International Association of Scientific, Technical and Medical Publishers; Association of American Publishers). The system was announced at the Frankfurt Book Fair 1997. The International DOI® Foundation (IDF) was created to develop and manage the DOI system, also in 1997. The DOI system was adopted as International Standard ISO 26324 in 2012 and updated in 2025. The DOI system implements the Handle System and adds a number of new features. The DOI system provides an infrastructure for persistent unique identification of objects of any type. The DOI system is designed to work over the Internet. A DOI name is permanently assigned to an object to provide a resolvable persistent network link to current information about that object, including where the object, or information about it, can be found on the Internet. While information about an object can change over time, its DOI name will not change. A DOI name can be resolved within the DOI system to values of one or more types of data relating to the object identified by that DOI name, such as a URL, an e-mail address, other identifiers and descriptive metadata. The DOI system enables the construction of automated services and transactions. Applications of the DOI system include but are not limited to managing information and documentation location and access; managing metadata; facilitating electronic transactions; persistent unique identification of any form of any data; and commercial and non-commercial transactions. The content of an object associated with a DOI name is described unambiguously by DOI metadata, based on a structured extensible data model that enables the object to be associated with metadata of any desired degree of precision and granularity to support description and services. The data model supports interoperability between DOI applications. The scope of the DOI system is not defined by reference to the type of content (format, etc.) of the referent, but by reference to the functionalities it provides and the context of use. The DOI system provides, within networks of DOI applications, for unique identification, persistence, resolution, metadata and semantic interoperability.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":\"https://doi.org/{$id}\"}]","contentType":"application/json","responseType":"SuccessTypeHintResponse","status":200}}},"testVariables":{"changed":false},"variables":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:43Z","entityUuid":"90958723-2ffa-4b75-80a5-354f433da8ee","parentUuid":"2563c25b-4c5c-4840-a3f4-09e5de5ded4f","uuid":"1086338e-10b9-48c6-a093-d58f806af27b"},{"content":{"allowCustomReply":{"changed":false},"annotations":{"changed":false},"eventType":"EditIntegrationEvent","integrationType":"ApiIntegration","name":{"changed":false},"requestAllowEmptySearch":{"changed":false},"requestBody":{"changed":false},"requestHeaders":{"changed":false},"requestMethod":{"changed":false},"requestUrl":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplate":{"changed":false},"responseItemTemplateForSelection":{"changed":false},"responseListField":{"changed":false},"testQ":{"changed":true,"value":"doi"},"testResponse":{"changed":true,"value":{"request":{"body":null,"headers":[{"key":"Accept","value":"application/json"},{"key":"Authorization","value":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiKey]"}],"method":"GET","url":"[SECRET:fairsharingApiUrl]/v2/search?registry=standard&record_type=identifier_schema&q=doi&status=ready"},"response":{"body":"[{\"id\":1332,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Digital Object Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.doi.org/the-identifier/resources/handbook/\",\"abbreviation\":\"DOI (ISO 26324:2025)\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.hFLKCn\",\"description\":\"The digital object identifier (DOI) system originated in a joint initiative of three trade associations in the publishing industry (International Publishers Association; International Association of Scientific, Technical and Medical Publishers; Association of American Publishers). The system was announced at the Frankfurt Book Fair 1997. The International DOI® Foundation (IDF) was created to develop and manage the DOI system, also in 1997. The DOI system was adopted as International Standard ISO 26324 in 2012 and updated in 2025. The DOI system implements the Handle System and adds a number of new features. The DOI system provides an infrastructure for persistent unique identification of objects of any type. The DOI system is designed to work over the Internet. A DOI name is permanently assigned to an object to provide a resolvable persistent network link to current information about that object, including where the object, or information about it, can be found on the Internet. While information about an object can change over time, its DOI name will not change. A DOI name can be resolved within the DOI system to values of one or more types of data relating to the object identified by that DOI name, such as a URL, an e-mail address, other identifiers and descriptive metadata. The DOI system enables the construction of automated services and transactions. Applications of the DOI system include but are not limited to managing information and documentation location and access; managing metadata; facilitating electronic transactions; persistent unique identification of any form of any data; and commercial and non-commercial transactions. The content of an object associated with a DOI name is described unambiguously by DOI metadata, based on a structured extensible data model that enables the object to be associated with metadata of any desired degree of precision and granularity to support description and services. The data model supports interoperability between DOI applications. The scope of the DOI system is not defined by reference to the type of content (format, etc.) of the referent, but by reference to the functionalities it provides and the context of use. The DOI system provides, within networks of DOI applications, for unique identification, persistence, resolution, metadata and semantic interoperability.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":\"https://doi.org/{$id}\"},{\"id\":4495,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"DDI Agency Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://registry.ddialliance.org/\",\"abbreviation\":null,\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.cfe09b\",\"description\":\"The DDI Agency Identifier is part of a free globally-unique identifier system for metadata-producing organizations. In concert with the DDI Agency Registry, the DDI Agency Identifier acts as both a namespace in a globally-unique id and a pointer for distributed service resolution.\\nBased on ISO/IEC 11179 Part 6 International Registration Data Identifiers (11179 IRDIs), DDI agency identifiers are used as the Registration Authority (11179 RA) in a IRDI and in DDI URNs.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":null},{\"id\":3904,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"Research Activity Identifier\",\"homepage\":\"https://raid.org/\",\"abbreviation\":\"RAiD\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.dc702a\",\"description\":\"Research Activity Identifier (RAiD) is an identifier mechanism designed for research projects and activities. It is persistent and connects existing PIDs for researchers, institutions, outputs and tools together to create a timeline of research activity which makes connecting digital infrastructure, reporting on impact and establishing data provenance clear and easy. RAiD is one of the Identifier Services owned and offered by the Australia Research Data Commons (ARDC), and can be accessed for machine-to-machine service by API integration, using a RAiD service point designated platform, or by manually minting via the RAiD user Dashboard. \",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":\"https://raid.org/{$id}\"},{\"id\":4538,\"type\":\"identifier_schema\",\"name\":\"International Generic Sample Number\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.igsn.org\",\"abbreviation\":\"IGSN (Generic)\",\"doi\":\"10.25504/FAIRsharing.c7f365\",\"description\":\"An IGSN ID is a globally unique and persistent identifier for physical samples in all scientific fields. An IGSN ID can be applied to an individual sample, an aggregation of samples, or to a feature-of-interest (the real-world feature that the sample is taken from). From 2021, IGSN IDs are functionally DOIs and are registered with metadata encoded in the DataCite Metadata Schema. The IGSN ID, previously known as an \\\"International GeoSample Number\\\", naming schema has changed, to reflects the expanding scope and utilization as a partnership agreement between DataCite and the IGSN e.V.\",\"registry\":\"standard\",\"status\":\"ready\",\"urlPattern\":\"http://igsn.org/{$id}\"}]","contentType":"application/json","responseType":"SuccessTypeHintResponse","status":200}}},"testVariables":{"changed":false},"variables":{"changed":false}},"createdAt":"2025-12-03T09:46:44Z","entityUuid":"90958723-2ffa-4b75-80a5-354f433da8ee","parentUuid":"2563c25b-4c5c-4840-a3f4-09e5de5ded4f","uuid":"2e261407-0d29-4e75-8ea7-6dbb97a8ec27"}],"forkOfPackageId":null,"id":"dmp.eosc.cz:czech-nrp-km:0.2.0","kmId":"czech-nrp-km","license":"Apache-2.0","mergeCheckpointPackageId":null,"metamodelVersion":20,"name":"Data Management Planning KM","nonEditable":false,"organizationId":"dmp.eosc.cz","phase":"ReleasedKnowledgeModelPackagePhase","previousPackageId":"dmp.eosc.cz:czech-nrp-km:0.1.7","readme":"# Data Management Planning KM\n\n*DSW Knowledge Model for Data Management Planning, tailored to the needs and specifics of the Czech National Repository Platform for Research Data.*\n\n## Maintainers\n\nResponsible for the overall management and development of the knowledge model, ensuring its continuous improvement, and overseeing contributions to maintain its integrity and relevance.\n\n* **Jana Martínková** \n  * [jana.martinkova@cvut.cz](mailto:jana.martinkova@cvut.cz)\n  * ORCID: [0000-0001-8575-6533](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8575-6533)\n  * *Czech Technical University in Prague*\n* **Kryštof Komanec** \n  * [krystof.komanec@fit.cvut.cz](mailto:krystof.komanec@fit.cvut.cz)\n  * ORCID: [0000-0003-3856-1682](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3856-1682) \n  * *Czech Technical University in Prague*\n\n## Contributors\n\nContribute to the knowledge model by adding new information, refining existing content, or providing insights to improve its quality and applicability.\n\n* **Marek Suchánek**, ORCID: [0000-0001-7525-9218](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7525-9218), *Czech Technical University in Prague*\n* **Georgia Koutentaki**, ORCID: [0000-0002-8099-9963](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8099-9963), *Czech Technical University in Prague*\n* **Michal Růžička**, ORCID: [0000-0001-5547-8720](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5547-8720), *Masaryk University*,  (as part of work on [FR CESNET 756/2024](https://www.muni.cz/en/research/projects/72488))\n* **Barbora Loskotová**, *Tomas Bata University in Zlín*, (as part of work on [FR CESNET 756/2024](https://www.muni.cz/en/research/projects/72488))\n* **Antonín Zita**, ORCID: [0009-0002-1049-107X](\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0002-1049-107X\n), *Masaryk University*, (as part of work on [FR CESNET 756/2024](https://www.muni.cz/en/research/projects/72488))\n* **Marika Hrubá**, *Masaryk University*, (as part of work on [FR CESNET 756/2024](https://www.muni.cz/en/research/projects/72488))\n* **Tereza Kolmačková**, *Library of the Czech Academy of Sciences*\n* **Jindřich Fejfar**, *Library of the Czech Academy of Sciences*\n* **Lucie Hošková**, ORCID: [0009-0001-9695-2342](https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9695-2342), *Charles University*\n* **Matěj Uchytil**, *Charles University*\n* **Anna Horecká**, *Charles University*\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\n| ![](https://dmp.eosc.cz/static/eu-msmt.svg) | This work is supported by the project National Repository Platform for Research Data no. [CZ.02.01.01/00/23_014/0008787](https://www.eosc.cz/en/projects/national-repository-platform-for-research-data-os-i-nrp/national-repository-platform). |\n|-------------------------------------|---|\n\n## Changelog\n\n### 0.2.0\n\n*Released*: 2026-24-03\n\n* Add tag \"CCMM\" to questions\n* Add and improve questions related to CCMM\n* Improve Other Outputs chapter\n* Updated FAIRsharing integration with record_type\n* Removed FAIRsharing integration for reused datasets name\n\n### 0.1.7\n\n*Released*: 2025-11-11\n\n* Add support for tagging content (approaches and strategies) as \"planned\"\n* Add tag \"Science Europe\" to questions\n* General updates based on feedback from KA7 NRP project\n\n### 0.1.6\n\n*Released*: 2025-16-10\n\n* Add annotations for document templates\n* Add tag \"maDMP\" to questions\n* General updates based on feedback from KA7 NRP project\n* Add resource links to Data Management Expert Guide\n\n### 0.1.5\n\n*Released*: 2025-24-07\n\n* Add annotations for document templates\n* Add tag \"Horizon Europe template\" to questions\n* General updates based on feedback from KA7 NRP project\n\n### 0.1.4\n\n*Released*: 2025-21-05\n\n* Refine the \"Producing Data\" chapter\n* Add phases to questions\n* General updates based on feedback from KA7 NRP project\n\n### 0.1.3\n\n*Released*: 2025-08-04\n\n* Integrate License Selector tool\n\n### 0.1.2\n\n*Released*: 2025-25-03\n\n* Refine the wording and order of the questions\n* Add a question for uploading the Data Sharing Agreement and Preservation Statement documents\n* Add URL question for measuring instrument, Data Sharing Agreement and Preservation Statement\n\n### 0.1.1\n\n*Released*: 2025-05-03\n\n* Add structure and preparation for License Selector\n* Add standard protocol and SW documentation\n* Add ORCID validation\n* Add \"Creator of DMP\" to roles\n* Improve integrations (link in response)\n\n### 0.1.0\n\n*Released*: 2025-08-01\n\n* Initial version for testing and further development","version":"0.2.0"}],"version":"0.2.0"}